id: design.file-pipeline
kind: design
title: File processing pipeline
aliases: file pipeline, batch files, etl, ingest files
tags: architecture, batch, data
questions:
  - how would you process a large set of uploaded files?
invariant: Files are ingested to durable storage first, then processed in bounded, resumable, idempotent stages keyed by a content address.
answer_30s: Upload to durable storage, enqueue a reference (not the bytes), process in bounded parallel stages keyed by a file digest, and make every stage resumable and safe to re-run.
answer_2m: |
  The shape follows from three constraints: files are large (so they must not
  travel through the message queue or memory), processing is slow (so it needs
  its own worker pool with bounded concurrency), and stages fail (so each stage
  must be resumable). Standard structure: direct upload to object storage with
  the queue carrying the object key or URL, a worker that streams the object,
  per-stage status recorded against the file digest, and outputs written to
  storage rather than passed in memory. Idempotency by content digest is what
  makes retries free: the same input file processed twice produces the same
  output, and a partially processed file is recognisable. Name the operational
  hooks: poison-file quarantine (so one bad file does not block the queue),
  per-stage metrics, and a dead-letter path.
when_to_use:
  - document ingest, media processing, data imports, log/file ETL
failure_modes:
  - file bytes in the queue payload, so the broker becomes a storage system
  - unbounded parallelism per file, so memory and IO collapse on large inputs
  - no poison-file handling, so the queue blocks behind one unprocessable item
  - no per-file state, so a restart reprocesses everything from the start
related:
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.idempotency
  - design.bounded-queue
  - rust.async-streams
  - rust.bounded-concurrency
  - recipe.file-processing-pipeline
sources:
  - https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/optimizing-performance.html
